        {
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "heading": "Saying no: ne... pas",
              "body": "To make a sentence negative, French wraps the verb in two little words: \"ne\" before the verb, \"pas\" after it. Je travaille → Je ne travaille pas. Before a vowel sound, \"ne\" becomes \"n'\": Je n'aime pas le café.",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "Je ne travaille pas le soir.", "english": "I don't work in the evening." },
                { "french": "Il ne regarde pas la télévision.", "english": "He doesn't watch television." },
                { "french": "Je n'aime pas le café.", "english": "I don't like coffee.", "note": "n' before a vowel" },
                { "french": "Nous ne dînons pas tard.", "english": "We don't have dinner late." },
                { "french": "Ils ne regardent jamais la télévision.", "english": "They never watch television.", "note": "ne... jamais = never" }
              ],
              "tip": "With reflexive verbs, the pronoun stays glued to the verb: Je ne me couche pas tard."
            }
          ]
        },
